{"kind":"assert","text":"Two questions from the cli failures on the cutover head (c79a409d, with main 64112e45 merged). The cli project fails 36 tests, and main passes all of them. 33 have clear fixes: test shape, and two behaviour faults that restore main's behaviour. Two questions need your ruling.\n\nQ1. Replay is stricter than the scope on a pinned definition (7 tests: application-local ×1, application-scenarios ×6). packages/replay/src/verify.ts:1522 (#pin) validates the pinned definition with `{ canonical: true }`. Compose commit 23e0c92d added that, and it also added canonical mode at :210, :405 and :2187. The scope admits the same Counter definition without canonical mode (packages/scope/src/core.ts:396). apps/counter/definition.json is not canonical (\"must omit redundant subjects and empty optional filters\"), so replay answers unsupported-definition for a room the scope accepted.\n(a) Drop canonical mode from replay's #pin and the other three sites. Replay then accepts what the scope admitted.\n(b) Apply 788bc90a at core.ts:396 too: the scope admits only canonical definitions into a new-format room. Re-spell apps/counter/definition.json with Define's normalize. Counter's digest changes, and I report it before the pin is committed, as for the demo Site.\nI recommend (b). 788bc90a says every call site that admits a definition into a new-format room passes canonical mode, and core.ts:396 looks like such a site. Replay and admission then agree on the stricter rule. If core.ts:396 is not an admission site in your sense, then (a).\n\nQ2. A forged source fact in a request now reads `incomplete`, where main read `mismatch` (verify.test.ts \"the source and the request\"). The cutover requires a retained copy for an anchored fact (verify.ts:2234, \"an anchored source lacks its original retained copy\") before replay compares the derived entry with the recorded one. Main compared first and answered mismatch. Both verdicts refuse the proof, but `mismatch` tells the reader that the history is inconsistent, and `incomplete` says that evidence is missing.\n(a) Keep the cutover order, and change the test expectation to `incomplete`. The note row names the verdict change.\n(b) Compare the derived entry before requiring the copy, so this case answers mismatch again.\nI recommend (b) if it does not move a ruled check. It keeps main's verdict for a forged request, and a reader acts differently on the two answers.\n\nRelated, for your information: the same tests showed that #privateCopy ignored facts proved through another room's result (#otherRoomResult). The fix accepts such a copy when its first entry checks against its birth, and the copy then enters `facts` under 3bf7e5aa (b). That is a behaviour fault, which the worker fixes with the second-room test as witness. In the relabel test, \"an invented source entry\" now reads mismatch where it read incomplete, which is a stronger verdict. The note lists it."}